facts:
A **part : part** ratio compares one group with the other group, e.g. pens : rulers $=1:3$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\foreach \x in {0,0.8} {\draw[fill=blue!25] (\x,0) rectangle ++(0.8,0.8);}
\foreach \x in {1.6,2.4,3.2} {\draw[fill=white] (\x,0) rectangle ++(0.8,0.8);}
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,mirror,amplitude=6pt}] (0,-0.15) -- (1.6,-0.15) node[midway,below=10pt] {$m$};
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,mirror,amplitude=6pt}] (1.6,-0.15) -- (4,-0.15) node[midway,below=10pt] {$n$};
\node at (2,-1.6) {part : part $=m:n$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **part : whole** ratio compares one group with the total instead of the other group, e.g. pens : all stationery $=1:4$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\foreach \x in {0,0.8} {\draw[fill=blue!25] (\x,0) rectangle ++(0.8,0.8);}
\foreach \x in {1.6,2.4,3.2} {\draw[fill=white] (\x,0) rectangle ++(0.8,0.8);}
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,mirror,amplitude=6pt}] (0,-0.15) -- (1.6,-0.15) node[midway,below=10pt] {$m$};
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,amplitude=6pt}] (0,0.95) -- (4,0.95) node[midway,above=10pt] {$m+n$};
\node at (2,-1.6) {part : whole $=m:m+n$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

